      modelAnswerHtml: 'Journey of the Magi does mark a genuine shift — unlike Prufrock or the hollow men, the Magus actually undergoes transformation, glimpsing renewal at the "temperate valley" and its baptismal imagery. But the poem stops well short of offering comfortable hope: the Magus returns "no longer at ease" among "an alien people clutching their gods", and closes by admitting he "should be glad of another death". The poem therefore offers hope only in a qualified, costly form — redemption is real, but it permanently estranges the Magus from his old life rather than simply resolving his difficulties.',
