  essayQuestions: [
    {
      id: 'essay-e-thesis-1',
      type: 'thesis',
      prompt: '"Eliot\'s poetry traces a single mind\'s journey from paralysis to redemption." To what extent is this true of the poems you have studied?',
      marks: null,
      suggestedEvidence: ['q-e-dare-disturb', 'q-e-bang-whimper', 'q-e-no-longer-at-ease'],
      modelThesisHtml:
        'Read as a body of work, Eliot\'s poems do trace a genuine arc from individual paralysis (Prufrock) through collective disillusionment (The Hollow Men) to a qualified, costly hope (Journey of the Magi) — but "redemption" understates how much that final poem insists on loss and estrangement as the price of spiritual change, rather than its opposite.',
      modelArgumentStructureHtml: 'Structure: (1) concede the genuine arc across the three poems (individual paralysis → collective paralysis → transformation); (2) complicate "redemption" specifically — the Magus\'s hope costs him his old life; (3) synthesise: Eliot\'s canon offers development, but refuses to make that development comfortable.',
      modelResponseHtml: 'A full response is not modelled at thesis-practice stage — see the full-essay question for a modelled response.',
      planningGuidanceHtml: 'Decode the question: "single mind\'s journey" is doing real work — a strong response should note that Eliot actually moves from an individual voice (Prufrock) to a collective one (The Hollow Men) before returning to an individual, if different, voice (the Magus) — the "journey" is not as simple or singular as the question implies.',
    },
    {
      id: 'essay-e-full-1',
      type: 'full-essay',
      prompt: 'Analyse how Eliot\'s formal choices — voice, structure and allusion — work together to shape his critique of modernity across the poems you have studied.',
      marks: 20,
      suggestedEvidence: ['q-e-etherised', 'q-e-coffee-spoons', 'q-e-dare-disturb', 'q-e-hollow-stuffed', 'q-e-the-shadow', 'q-e-bang-whimper', 'q-e-cold-coming', 'q-e-no-longer-at-ease'],
      modelThesisHtml:
        'Eliot\'s critique of modernity is inseparable from his formal choices — dramatic monologue and fragmented syntax do not simply describe psychological paralysis, they enact it for the reader, while his shift toward allusive, retrospective form in Journey of the Magi enacts an equally formal shift toward the possibility, though not the ease, of redemption.',
      modelArgumentStructureHtml: 'Suggested structure: (1) intro with thesis; (2) Prufrock — dramatic monologue and fragmented syntax formally enacting individual paralysis; (3) The Hollow Men — collective voice and anaphoric repetition generalising that paralysis, resolved through deliberate anticlimax; (4) Journey of the Magi — the shift to retrospective, allusive dramatic monologue formally enacting a hard-won, costly transformation; (5) conclusion on how form and idea are inseparable across the whole body of work.',
      modelResponseHtml:
        '<p>T.S. Eliot\'s critique of modernity across his early and middle poetry is never simply a matter of what his speakers say about the modern world — it is built into how the poems are constructed. Dramatic monologue, fragmented syntax, personification and allusion are not decorative techniques layered over a straightforward argument; they are the mechanism by which Eliot makes psychological paralysis, collective disillusionment and eventual, costly transformation legible to a reader, rather than simply describing these states from the outside.</p><p>"The Love Song of J. Alfred Prufrock" establishes this method from its opening simile, comparing the evening to "a patient etherised upon a table" — a jarringly clinical image that immediately rejects Romantic convention and signals that Eliot\'s modern world offers no comforting beauty. The poem\'s dramatic monologue form places the reader directly inside Prufrock\'s anxious consciousness, and its fragmented syntax formally enacts his hesitation: "Do I dare / Disturb the universe?" breaks across the line exactly where Prufrock\'s nerve breaks, so the reader experiences suspended uncertainty rather than being told about it. Even Prufrock\'s central symbol — "I have measured out my life with coffee spoons" — works through compression rather than statement, reducing an entire wasted life to a single small, repeated domestic object.</p><p>"The Hollow Men" extends this same formal logic from an individual to a collective scale. Where Prufrock speaks in a singular, anxious "I", the hollow men speak only as "we" — "We are the hollow men / We are the stuffed men" — and the droning anaphoric repetition of this opening enacts the very emptiness it describes, stripping the collective of any individuating voice at all. This formal generalisation continues through the poem\'s central motif: the capitalised "Shadow" that "falls" between four separate binary pairings (idea/reality, motion/act, conception/creation, desire/existence) uses repeated structure to insist that the gap between intention and action is total, not occasional. The poem\'s famous closing anticlimax — "Not with a bang but a whimper" — is the logical formal conclusion of this pattern: a poem built entirely from repetition and negation can only end in deflation, never in dramatic resolution, because dramatic resolution would require exactly the decisive action the hollow men are formally incapable of.</p><p>"Journey of the Magi", written after Eliot\'s 1927 conversion to Anglicanism, marks a genuine formal shift that mirrors its thematic one. Where Prufrock and the hollow men speak from inside their own unresolved present, the Magus narrates retrospectively, opening with an allusion to a real 1622 Nativity sermon — "A cold coming we had of it" — that immediately establishes distance and reflection rather than raw, unresolved anxiety. This formal detachment allows Eliot to represent genuine transformation: the Magus recalls a real, concrete vision of renewal at "a temperate valley… with a running stream and a water-mill", its baptismal imagery working through allusion exactly as Prufrock\'s clinical simile or the hollow men\'s scarecrow imagery did earlier. Yet Eliot refuses to let this formal shift resolve into simple comfort. The poem\'s closing lines — the Magus "no longer at ease here… with an alien people clutching their gods" — use the same technique of concrete, allusive imagery to insist that transformation has cost him his old life entirely, not simply improved it.</p><p>Across all three poems, then, Eliot\'s formal choices are never separable from his ideas about modernity. Fragmented syntax and dramatic monologue enact individual paralysis; anaphoric repetition and collective voice generalise that paralysis into a post-war condition; and a shift toward retrospective, allusive form enacts a transformation that is genuine but costly. Eliot\'s critique of modernity, in other words, is not something his poems argue — it is something they formally perform.</p>',
      planningGuidanceHtml: 'Decode the question: "voice, structure and allusion" gives you a built-in three-part structure across the three poems — use it deliberately rather than discussing technique and theme separately. For each poem, name the SPECIFIC formal choice and explain how it enacts (not just describes) that poem\'s particular stage in the paralysis-to-redemption arc.',
    },
  ],
